This ascension, the first of two (so far), took place on June 5th, 2006.  
I have another character who is about to ascend (which will be my third),
but I decided to hold off on that ascension until I wrote my YAFAP post.

I was playing Nethack on and off for about three months.  Actually, during
that time, I had uninstalled the game, reinstalled it, uninstalled it, and
reinstalled it.  The main reason for uninstalling it?  It was taking up
way too much of my time.  The main reason for reinstalling it?  I couldn't
get it off my mind.  My wife (fiancee at the time) admits that she doubted
that Nethack was gone forever, and she wasn't at all surprised when it
went back on my Nokia 770.

Since starting to play Nethack in the beginning of the year, I've been
playing it exclusively on the Nokia 770 Internet Tablet
(www.nokia.com/770/).  It's a GTK-based Nethack port, and I have yet to
play Nethack on a console user interface.

I don't recall all the items that Aaron the Wizard started off with, but I
do recall starting off with a spellbook of identify, which was extremely
useful.  In addition, relatively early on in the game, I found some
gauntlets of power, which made it a lot easier to survive a battle as a
weak wizard, and I tended to fight rather than run.  I continued to wear
the gauntlets till about 3/4 through the game, and then I exchanged them
for leather gloves because I was tired of occasionally failing to cast
some spells as a result of the metal in the gauntlets.  Even with the
gauntlets, I was still able to cast magic missile with a 0% failure rate,
and as that was my most useful spell, I stuck it out with the gauntlets
for some time.

As this was my first ascension, I managed to make quite a few mistakes.  
My first major mistake occurred after I found a bag of holding in Sokoban.  
I was so elated to have even made it to the end of Sokoban (this was the
first time I had gotten this far) that I started stuffing all the items
that I didn't have an immediate need for in the BoH, including various
wands... including an already identified wand of cancellation.  I had
briefly read the spoiler about what not to put in a BoH, which also made
sense from my knowledge of D&D, but, well, I was careless.  When I lost
the BoH, I was so disappointed, and I considered quitting the game and
starting another game.  But, I decided to continue, considering that I had
never gotten this far in a Nethack game (and I had played many games by
this point) and that my character was very much alive.  I didn't find
another BoH until much later in the game (well, I wished for it using the
Castle wand of wishing, but soon after I found one anyway), and as a
result, I ended up using a couple of equipment stashes to manage my
inventory.  Needless to say, I've been extremely careful with BoHs since
making this mistake.

Twice I lost my god-given protection.  Both times I was playing the game
late at night, misread a message, and prayed at the wrong time.  However,
the second time I lost the protection, I was a level 30 character, and I
had to spend quite a bit of time scouring the dungeon for gold, especially
in Gehennom, and carting the gold back up to the Minetown priest.  It was
a real pain, and I haven't repeated this mistake since.

At one point, I lost my telepathy intrinsic after I killed a peaceful
human priest.  I had gotten the "brilliant" idea that I would kill some
priests for their spellbooks (I was looking for a spellbook of create
monster, which I did find on the body of one of the dead priests).  So, I
would stand in a temple of one of the cross-aligned priests and cast
finger of death with a 0% failure rate at the priest.  The priest's god
would be displeased, send some lightning at me, etc.  Actually, at the
time, I probably thought that my god would be happy, as I was killing
cross-aligned priests and converting their altars.  But, soon enough, I
realized that I had lost the telepathy intrinsic, and I had to scour the
dungeons for a floating eye that left a body.

I didn't understand the business of offering bodies on an altar until
after I completed the quest.  In reading the spoilers, I saw Magicbane
being mentioned several times, and that Wizards get it as their first
weapon, but I interpreted what I read as meaning I would get it when
praying on an altar.  I prayed on altars as often as I could, but
Magicbane simply would not appear.  I was crowned before I ever sacrificed
anything to my god, and I completed the quest with a +7 Quarterstaff.  I
started to wonder if there was a bug in my version of Nethack that
prevented me from getting Magicbane.  Once I understood what was really
involved in sacrificing, I used up all my scrolls of create monster and
wands of create monster, and on the last charge of the last wand, I got a
monster that left a body, sacrificed it, and got Magicbane!

Where I made the most serious and life-threatening mistakes was on the
Astral Plane, but I'll describe my harrowing endgame ordeal a bit later.

I think I started with a pet cat, who continued to function as a cat until
one or two levels below Minetown.  On this level, I used a wand of secret
door detection, and it uncovered a polymorph trap.  As soon as the various
monsters noticed the trap, they started throwing themselves on it, and I
soon found myself faced with more dangerous monsters.  I eventually put a
stop to it by pushing a boulder onto the trap, but then I had the idea to
cause my pet to step on the trap, and I eventually ended up with a white
dragon.  I informally named the white dragon Frosty, but I didn't
officially name my pet (via the command) until much later on in the game.  
Frosty stayed with me until a little time after I first entered Gehennom,
and he was killed by a red dragon when I wasn't paying attention.  I
killed the red dragon in vengeance and tamed a black dragon, but it just
wasn't the same without Frosty, and I soon tired of the black dragon,
abandoned it, and came back some time later after it had gone native and
killed it.

The Quest was pretty easy.  I ended up dueling it out with the Dark One,
and as I was making my way back up to the Quest Home Level, I discovered
that my blindfold, which I had been wearing during the battle, had been
cursed and could not be removed.  I don't think I knew remove curse at the
time, and as I was unable to read my scrolls of remove curse, I was in a
bit of a bind.  Luckily, I was safely able to pray at this point, and my
god uncursed the blindfold.

I encountered both the Rogue Level and Fort Ludios (the gold there was
quite useful in getting back my lost protection points), but no Big Room.  
The Castle was a breeze, a silver dragon left some scales for me, and then
I had a wand of wishing.  What to wish for?  I mostly had everything I
needed, and I felt that it wasn't right to wish for something that I
hadn't already seen.  While I might have known about a certain item from
reading the spoilers, how was my character to actually know about an item
until encountering one?  So, while I did wish for things (a BoH, and I
think some magic markers) and didn't get the wishless conduct, I did have
an untracked voluntary conduct to not wish for something that my character
hadn't seen or would be smart enough to deduce (for example, sometimes
dragons leave scales, and even if a silver dragon that one kills does not
leave scales, it's easy to determine that the dragon reflects ray attacks,
and as such, its scales would likely do the same).

At Moloch's Sanctum, I encountered a priest with Grayswandir, which I 
claimed as my own and replaced Firebrand with (never did get Frostbrand).  
After climbing back up, I spent some time sacrificing and using 
Grayswandir till I had basic ability, and then I was on to the Elemental 
Planes.  I had deliberately not read much about the End Game, and I was 
in for some surprises.

Air was a pain, but I used teleport away and finger of death to eliminate
the air elemental threat.  Water was much harder than it needed to be.  I
didn't understand that I could levitate over the water, so I stuck to the
air bubble and kept away from the various creatures that would have loved
to drag me to a watery grave.  Using my crystal ball, I continued to
search for traps, and I didn't understand why the portal kept on moving
around, but eventually, the air bubbles met up, and I quickly made it over
to the portal.

Ah, the Astral Plane.  I understood from my brief perusal of the spoilers
that I needed to offer the amulet on my god's high altar, and I knew some
of the details of the riders, but that was about it.  I think the layout
was Famine on the left, Pestilence in the middle, and Death on the right,
but Pestilence and Death may have been switched.  First thing I did was go
to the middle, and one of the first enemies out the door was a
cross-aligned priest.  So, I thought, obviously the middle cannot be the
right way--it must be for a cross-aligned god.  Needless to say, this was
a significant error on my part.  And, as a result of these actions, I now
had Pestilence on my tail.

I went to the right and encountered Death.  I dueled it out with Death a
couple of times, and I noticed that my wands of magic missiles weren't
doing very much, so I switched over to casting magic missiles... while
holding the Amulet of Yendor.

I continued to battle monsters (for some reason, I wasn't thinking
straight and was not teleporting them away), with both Pestilence and
Death tracking me, and I eventually found myself in the corridor between
the right altar and the room leading to the altar.  On my right:  a whole
host of monsters, starting with an iron golem.  On my immediate left:
Pestilence and then Death!  I smacked the iron golem, and Pestilence
hit--I cast Cure Sickness.  Pestilence hit again--I cast Cure Sickness.  
And Pestilence hit again, and I tried to cast Cure Sickness, but I didn't
have enough spell energy to do so!  I had used up some spell energy on the
Elemental Planes, and I had used a ton up casting magic missile and other
spells on the Astral Plane, and now I was in serious trouble.  I prayed to
my god, and my god healed me.  I wasn't sure what to do then.  If I used
one of my wands of death to kill Pestilence, it was going to make Death
super-strong.  Plus, my guardian angel was also to the left, and I didn't
want to anger him.  If I used a wand of death or teleport away to my
right, it might have struck a peaceful priest, perhaps even my own high
priest, and I didn't want that either.

I needed to make a decision, so I struck the iron golem, and Pestilence
hit again.  I furiously reached into my bag of holding, grabbed a blessed
potion of extra healing, and quaffed it, phew.  But, I only had a few more
healing-style potions, and I needed to change my strategy.  On the left,
things had changed a little bit.  My guardian angel was no longer in the
way, and I decided that I could live with a super-strong Death, just so
long as I (temporarily) eliminated Pestilence, and I zapped my wand of
death as Pestilence and Death and Pestilence dropped dead.

Through a combination of wands and brute force, I made it through to the
right altar, with Death permanently chomping away at my hit points.  
Unfortunately, it wasn't my god's altar.  In addition, I made an important
discovery at this time--I no longer had Magicbane, and I had been slapping
around monsters with my bare hands for who knows how long.  Most likely, a
monster had a bullwhip had ripped the weapon right out of my character's
hand (I know this is possible based on a recent game that I played).  
Now, theoretically, I should have discovered this earlier, but the
messages were scrolling by fast and furious, and I didn't notice it until
I checked my inventory.

I switched to Grayswandir (curses be damned), and prepared to wade back 
through a sea of monsters that had accumulated as a result of opening the 
middle door, changing my mind, and heading over to the right.  Plus, 
Pestilence had arisen from the dead, and Death was also in the way.

Somehow, I made it to the right door without allowing Pestilence to touch
me again, although Death wasn't playing as nice.  Or rather, I made it to
right in front of the door entrance/bottleneck--there were tons of
monsters here, and Pestilence was closing in.  At one point, I remember
using my wand of teleport on both Pestilence and Death (Pestilence must
have been the one next to me--I can't think of any other reason to do
this).  I was surrounded on all sides, and there was only room for one of
them to appear next to me.  I guess it was Death that appeared next to me,
and Pestilence was quite a bit aways.

I made it through the door, and things started to get easier.  I put some 
distance between myself and the riders and made it to the middle altar, 
which was my god's altar.  I could have spent some time uncursing all my 
cursed items or searching for Magicbane, but I was weary from the intense, 
life-threatening battle, and I was ready to ascend.  I placed the Amulet 
of Yendor on the altar and tried to offer it, but that didn't work.  A 
quick search through the newsgroups gave me the proper education, and 
picked it up and offered it again, and I was successful:

